<BaseLayout title="Pyramids, Iguanas and a Sea Turtle — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A week in July 2022 on the Yucatán Peninsula with my wife and a group of friends: Cancún's Hotel Zone, a long day out to Chichén Itzá, a cenote and Valladolid, a night at Coco Bongo, then Tulum's cliff-top ruins and a green sea turtle in the shallows.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · July 2022</p>
  <h1>Pyramids, Iguanas and a Sea Turtle</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A week on the Yucatán Peninsula with friends: the beach at Cancún, two Maya cities, a cenote, and Tulum.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>7 days · July 24–30, 2022</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → over Holbox → Cancún Hotel Zone → Kaua → Chichén Itzá → a cenote near Ebtún → Valladolid →
    Coco Bongo → down the coast to Tulum → the Tulum ruins and a snorkel off the coast → Cancún airport → Los Angeles<br />
    Two Mexican states, Quintana Roo and Yucatán · two Maya cities, one inland and one on a cliff · one cenote · two dolphins in
    the air at once · more iguanas than anyone counted · one green sea turtle
  </div>

  <p>
    In the summer of 2022, after two years in which nobody went anywhere, a group of friends decided we should all
    go somewhere at once, and the somewhere was Cancún. My wife and I flew down from Los Angeles on a Sunday morning
    in late July, and for a week we did what people do on the Yucatán Peninsula. We spent some days doing nothing
    much by the water. On others we got up in the dark to go and look at very old stones.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.jpg'} alt="Map of the trip on the Yucatán Peninsula: a flight past Holbox to Cancún, a day trip west to Chichén Itzá and Valladolid and back, then south along the coast to Tulum." /></a>
    <figcaption>The week on the peninsula, in the order the photos were taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    As with the other trips on this page, everything below is pieced together from the camera roll, with the time and
    place each photo was taken. It was a group trip, so many of the best photos are of people. Those stay in the
    family album, and what's left here is the scenery, the ruins, the animals and a fair amount of food. Where the
    photos are silent I've said so rather than guess.
  </p>

  <h2>Days 1 and 2: Down to the Caribbean</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0724.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0724.jpg'} alt="Map of Days 1 and 2: a flight from LAX across Mexico, past Holbox island to Cancún, and an evening on the beach at Punta Cancún." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 1–2: across Mexico by air, then a sunset on the beach.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Holbox from the window</h3>
  <p>
    The first photo of the trip is from seven in the morning at LAX, and the next good one is from the plane at 3:31
    in the afternoon, local time, as we came in over the top of the peninsula. Below the wing was
    <strong>Holbox</strong>, a long, thin island off the northern tip of Quintana Roo, separated from the mainland by
    a shallow lagoon. From above it looks like a white hook of sand laid on green and turquoise water. The island has no
    cars to speak of, only golf carts and sandy streets, and the waters around it are part of the Yum Balam reserve,
    where whale sharks gather in summer. We never set foot on it. It just happened to be under the window.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'holbox-from-the-air.jpg'} alt="View from an airplane window of Holbox island: a curving white sand spit, green mangroves and shallow turquoise water, the wing at the top." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Holbox, a hook of sand at the top of the Yucatán, from the plane.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By five we were on the ground and checked in at an all-inclusive resort on the Hotel Zone. The
    <strong>Hotel Zone</strong> is Cancún's strange and famous geography: a narrow barrier island shaped like the
    number 7, with the Caribbean on one side and the Nichupté lagoon on the other, and hotels shoulder to shoulder
    along all of it. Fifty-odd years ago almost none of it existed. In the late 1960s the Mexican government chose
    this nearly empty sandbar, with a fishing village nearby, as the site of a planned resort city, and built it more
    or less from scratch. We were near the bend of the 7, at <strong>Punta Cancún</strong>. From the room we looked
    down on a pool laid out in terraces and then the sea.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-zone-pool.jpg'} alt="View from a hotel room over a terraced blue pool, palms and lawn to the turquoise Caribbean under a blue sky with clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view on the first evening, a little after six.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>An evening on the beach</h3>
  <p>
    Monday has no daytime photos at all, which I think is an accurate record of what we did. The camera comes back out
    at a quarter to eight in the evening, on the beach at Punta Cancún, where the sun was setting behind the hotel
    towers and the sand had turned gray-blue. The beaches on this side of the point face north into the bay rather
    than out to the open sea, so the water is calm, and plenty of people were still swimming in the last of the light.
    Some of us went in too.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'punta-cancun-sunset.jpg'} alt="Dusk on a wide sandy beach at Punta Cancún, a lit hotel tower on the right and a yellow sunset behind palm trees and low buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset at Punta Cancún, Monday, 7:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 3: Chichén Itzá, a cenote and Valladolid</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0726.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0726.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 3: from Cancún west across the Yucatán to Kaua, Chichén Itzá, a cenote near Ebtún and Valladolid, then back to Punta Cancún for the night." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: two hundred kilometers inland and back, and a late night.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Tuesday was the big day. The first photo is from 7:13 in the morning, a hurried breakfast plate, and by eleven we
    were far to the west on a day tour, in the state of Yucatán. The road from Cancún runs dead straight
    through low, dense scrub forest. The Yucatán is a flat slab of limestone, and there is hardly a hill or a river on
    it. The first stop the camera recorded was <strong>Kaua</strong>, a small Maya village just off the old road, where the photo shows a
    community center with a carved stone disc set in its wall.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'kaua.jpg'} alt="A low wall with a large carved circular stone emblem and a painted mural beside a gate, under a cloudy sky, in the village of Kaua." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The community center at Kaua, 10:56 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Chichén Itzá</h3>
  <p>
    By noon we were at <strong>Chichén Itzá</strong>. It was one of the great cities of the Maya world, at its height
    roughly a thousand years ago, and it is now one of the most visited places in Mexico. A UNESCO World Heritage Site
    since 1988, it was voted one of the "New Seven Wonders of the World" in 2007. At the center of a huge, flat lawn
    stands <strong>El Castillo</strong>, the Temple of Kukulcán: nine stepped terraces with a staircase up each side
    and a small temple on top. The building is also a calendar. Each staircase has 91 steps, and the four together
    plus the top platform make 365. At the spring and autumn equinoxes, the late-afternoon sun throws a zigzag of
    shadow down the edge of the northern stairs, so that a serpent of light appears to crawl down to the stone snake's
    head at the bottom.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'el-castillo.jpg'} alt="El Castillo at Chichén Itzá, a huge stepped limestone pyramid with a staircase up its face, on a green lawn under a pale, overcast sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>El Castillo, the Temple of Kukulcán.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    You can no longer climb it; that ended in 2006. So you walk around it with everyone else, in a crowd of tour
    groups following guides with umbrellas. July in the Yucatán is the rainy season, and the sky was full of heavy
    clouds that afternoon, with puddles on the lawns. East of the pyramid is the <strong>Temple of the
    Warriors</strong>, a stepped platform fronted by rows and rows of square stone columns. Once they held up a roof;
    now they stand in ranks in the grass like a petrified army.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'temple-of-the-warriors.jpg'} alt="The Temple of the Warriors at Chichén Itzá, a broad stepped platform with stone columns, under dark storm clouds, with visitors on the wet lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Temple of the Warriors, under rain clouds, 1:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Down into a cenote</h3>
  <p>
    Because the Yucatán is limestone, rain doesn't run off in rivers. It sinks through the rock and flows underground,
    and where the roof of a cave has fallen in you get a <strong>cenote</strong>: a round, sheer-sided pit
    with fresh water at the bottom. To the Maya they were wells, and also openings to the underworld. Our cenote
    stop, at 3:41, was a little west of Valladolid, near the village of Ebtún. From the rim it looked like a giant
    stone drum, its walls hung with ferns and roots reaching down to the water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cenote-rim.jpg'} alt="Looking down into a deep round cenote from its stone rim, with plants and flowers growing on the ledge and dark water far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the rim.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cenote-stairs.jpg'} alt="A long wooden staircase zigzagging down the wall of a cenote to platforms by the water, where swimmers in life vests are gathered." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The staircase down to the water.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A wooden staircase zigzags down the wall to platforms at water level, where people in life vests were jumping in.
    After a morning on the hot lawns of Chichén Itzá, the water at the bottom of a cenote is the best thing in the
    Yucatán.
  </p>
  <p>
    The last stop, around half past five, was <strong>Valladolid</strong>, a colonial town founded by the Spanish in
    the 1540s on top of a Maya town, with a big church on its main square. The only photos from there are of the
    paperwork of the tour, so it gets a mention and no picture. Then came the long drive back to the coast.
  </p>

  <h3>Coco Bongo</h3>
  <p>
    That should have been enough for one day, but we had tickets for that night. Punta Cancún is also the Hotel
    Zone's party district, a cluster of clubs and bars within a few hundred meters of each other. By 11:25 pm we were
    on the strip by Fat Tuesday, and just before midnight we were inside <strong>Coco Bongo</strong>, which is part
    nightclub and part stage show. The videos from that night are mostly noise and light: acrobats flying over the
    crowd on wires, dancers on the balconies, and a hall packed to the walls. The last clip is from 12:21 am.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coco-bongo.jpg'} alt="Inside a packed nightclub hall bathed in purple and green light, an aerial performer hanging in the spotlights above the crowd and balconies." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An aerialist over the crowd at Coco Bongo, a quarter to midnight (a frame from a video).</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Days 4 and 5: Dolphins, a convertible, and south to Tulum</h2>
  <p>
    Wednesday was a day of recovery, and the photos show it: breakfast a little after nine, a bowl of fruit and granola
    and an everything bagel with smoked salmon, then nothing until dinner.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'breakfast-bowl.jpg'} alt="A bowl of melon, pineapple, strawberries, raspberries and blueberries on granola." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wednesday breakfast.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'breakfast-pancakes.jpg'} alt="Breakfast from above: rolled tacos with pico de gallo and guacamole, pancakes with berries, a small bowl of berries and a box of cornflakes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Thursday breakfast: tacos, pancakes, and cornflakes.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Two dolphins in the air</h3>
  <p>
    The resort has a dolphin lagoon by the beach, and at some point in those middle days we went in, in blue life
    vests, with the trainers. The photographs are the professional kind, and they carry no real time, so I can't
    say exactly when. Most of them are portraits, which stay private. My favorite is the moment the trainers sent two
    dolphins up out of the water together, side by side, in front of the palms.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dolphins-leap.jpg'} alt="Two bottlenose dolphins leaping out of the water side by side in a lagoon, with palm trees and a beach cabana behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Two dolphins, one leap.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Lunch in a convertible</h3>
  <p>
    On Thursday we packed up. Before we left the Hotel Zone there was lunch at a 1950s-style diner where the booths are
    old cars: you sit in the seats of a turquoise convertible with chrome trim and whitewall tires, and the table is
    where the dashboard should be.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'convertible-booth.jpg'} alt="The side of a turquoise 1950s convertible with chrome trim, used as a booth in a diner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The booth.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0728.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0728.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 5: from Cancún's Hotel Zone south along the coast to the Tulum beach road." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 5: down the coast to Tulum.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Then we went south. Tulum is an hour or two down the coast from Cancún, past Playa del Carmen, on the road that
    runs along what is now sold as the Riviera Maya. Nobody photographed the trip down. The next picture is dinner at
    7:16 that evening, on the <strong>beach road</strong> south of town, where the hotels and restaurants hide in the
    jungle between the road and the sea. A steak arrived on a board, there were candles on the table, and orange drinks in tall glasses.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'steak.jpg'} alt="A grilled steak, sliced, on a dark wooden board in warm candlelight." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dinner on the Tulum beach road, 7:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 6: Tulum</h2>
  <p>
    Friday started late, with lunch on the beach road at noon: shrimp on a crisp tostada, with tomato, red cabbage and a
    little glass of green salsa.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0729.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0729.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 6 in Tulum: lunch on the beach road, the ruins entrance, the archaeological zone and El Castillo, the snorkel spot off Playa Maya, and dinner back on the beach road." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 6: lunch, the ruins, the sea below them, and dinner.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    By three we were at the entrance to the ruins, where a set of giant red letters spelling TULUM, with "Mágico"
    underneath, stands next to a giant fiberglass ice-cream cone.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shrimp-tostada.jpg'} alt="A shrimp tostada topped with tomato, red cabbage and a drizzle of sauce on a gray plate, with a small glass of green salsa." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Shrimp tostada, 12:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulum-magico.jpg'} alt="Big red letters spelling TULUM on a white base reading Mágico, beside a giant fiberglass ice-cream cone." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sign at the entrance.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The walled city on the cliff</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Tulum</strong> is small next to Chichén Itzá, but it has the best setting of any Maya city. It sits on a
    limestone cliff right above the Caribbean, enclosed on its three landward sides by a stone wall. "Tulum" means
    "wall"; its older name was probably Zamá, "dawn", since it faces east over the sea. It was at its peak late, in
    the thirteenth to fifteenth centuries, as a trading port for canoes working the coast, and it was still a living
    town when Spanish ships first sailed past in 1518. It was an overcast, windy afternoon, and the gray stone looked
    right under a gray sky.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'great-palace.jpg'} alt="A long ruined stone palace with broken walls on a green lawn at Tulum, under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One of the palaces inside the wall.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulum-temple.jpg'} alt="A small stone temple with a thatched shelter on its roof, on a raised platform across a wide lawn, with an interpretive sign in the grass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A temple across the lawn.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The ruins have another population: iguanas. Black spiny-tailed iguanas, some of them a meter long, lie
    everywhere on the warm stones and in the grass, and they let you get very close before they bother to move. One
    lay at the foot of a wall with its back to a row of old columns, as if it owned the place, which in a sense it does.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'iguana-closeup.jpg'} alt="Close-up of a gray, banded black spiny-tailed iguana lying in grass and dry leaves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A black spiny-tailed iguana.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'iguana-columns.jpg'} alt="A large iguana resting in the grass at the foot of a ruined stone wall, with stone columns behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An iguana among the columns.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The building everyone comes for is <strong>El Castillo</strong>, a temple on the highest point of the cliff,
    with palms around it and the sea behind. The classic view is from a path along the cliff, and at 3:52 that
    path was lined with people holding up phones, us included.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulum-castillo.jpg'} alt="The El Castillo temple at Tulum on a rocky cliff above the sea, surrounded by palms, under a dramatic blue and white sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>El Castillo on its cliff, 3:52 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Walk the other way along the cliff edge and you look down on surf breaking over the rocks, and on a wooden
    staircase down to a small beach under the ruins.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulum-surf.jpg'} alt="Gray-green sea with white surf breaking along a rocky, scrub-covered shore below the Tulum ruins." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The coast north of the ruins.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulum-beach-stairs.jpg'} alt="Looking down from the cliff to a beach with waves, two small boats offshore, and a wooden staircase leading down to the sand." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The staircase down to the beach, 4:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>A turtle in the shallows</h3>
  <p>
    An hour later we were in the water ourselves, snorkeling off the coast near Tulum, with masks and a phone in a
    waterproof case. Over a bottom of sand and sea grass, the first thing we saw was
    a pair of southern stingrays, flat and sand-colored, gliding just above the floor. Then a <strong>green sea
    turtle</strong> came past, grazing on the sea grass and paying no attention to us at all. Green turtles nest on
    these beaches in summer, and they feed in the grass beds close to shore.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stingrays.jpg'} alt="Two southern stingrays over a sandy, grassy bottom in green water, seen from above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Southern stingrays, 5:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sea-turtle.jpg'} alt="A green sea turtle swimming over sea grass in clear turquoise water, its patterned shell close to the camera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A green sea turtle, a minute later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It swam slowly off into the blue, its flippers barely moving.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sea-turtle-2.jpg'} alt="A green sea turtle seen from above swimming away over a pale sandy bottom in turquoise water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Swimming away.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back on land, ten minutes later, there was one more animal: a spider monkey walking along the top of a wall,
    its long tail curled up into a question mark behind it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'spider-monkey.jpg'} alt="A spider monkey walking along the top of a white wall against a pale sky, its long tail curled up behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A spider monkey on a wall, 5:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was late, after nine, back on the beach road. Dessert was a long platter by candlelight, with fried
    plantains, scoops of ice cream under caramel, shredded coconut, and a tall glass of fruit in the middle. Later, a
    little after eleven, the last night out of the trip ended at Taboo, where the waiters carry sparklers through the
    dining room.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tulum-dinner.jpg'} alt="A long dark platter by candlelight with fried plantains, scoops of ice cream drizzled with caramel, shredded coconut and a tall glass of fruit in the center." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dessert on the beach road, 9:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 7: Home</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0730.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0730.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 7: from Tulum up the coast to Cancún airport, then the flight home across Mexico to Los Angeles." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 7: up the coast to the airport, and home.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Saturday morning, and the hour or two from Tulum back to the airport at Cancún, went unrecorded. Our flight left at 5:40 in
    the afternoon, and the camera only comes back out at the other end, at dusk, as the plane came down over the
    eastern edge of Los Angeles. From up there the city at night is a grid of orange streetlights running to the
    horizon, with the dark lines of freeways cutting across it. We were due in at LAX at 8:43 pm, in a city two hours behind the Caribbean.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'la-descent.jpg'} alt="View from a plane window at dusk: a lit wingtip over the sprawling lights of the San Gabriel Valley." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Over the San Gabriel Valley, 8:27 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'la-grid.jpg'} alt="The street grid of Los Angeles at night from a descending plane, orange lights to the horizon under the wing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The grid, two minutes later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Ruins, reptiles and the sea</h2>
  <p>
    Lined up in order, the week is a set of contrasts. There's a planned resort city built on a sandbar fifty years
    ago, and two Maya cities that were old long before anyone thought of it. One of those is a pyramid that tells the
    time on an inland plain, the other a small walled port on a cliff. Underground there was a well you climb down a
    wooden staircase to swim in. Out in the water were two dolphins in the air at once and a turtle that ignored us
    completely. Ashore, the iguanas had taken over a city the Maya left centuries ago.
  </p>
  <p>
    But mostly it was a week with friends, and those photos are not on this page. What's here is what the rest of us
    saw around them: the hook of Holbox under the wing, a stone serpent's staircase under rain clouds, gray stone on a gray
    afternoon above a gray-green sea, and the lights of home coming up under the wing at the end.
  </p>

  <p class="muted"><small>Maps built from the trip's photo timestamps and locations. Photo locations have been removed
    from the published images; group photos are left out, and some photos are cropped to leave people out. Day maps:
    map data © OpenStreetMap contributors; each stop is placed where its photo was taken, hotels and home are never
    marked, and the dashed lines show the order of stops, not the exact roads or flight paths.</small></p>
